Now I have all the information needed. Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** for `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`.

---

## Integration-Test Context

This class is unambiguously integration-scoped:
- Class name contains `Integration`
- Decorated with `[TestCategory("Integration")]`
- Project name is `DataAccess.IntegrationTests`

Per calibration rules, **Mystery Guest** and **Eager Test** severity is downgraded for this class. **Sleepy Test** remains high even for integration tests.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 4 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **7** |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — 1. Sleepy Test

**Academic name:** Sleepy Test  
**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82–90)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // line 87 — blocks for 3 seconds
```

Even in integration tests, a hard `Thread.Sleep` is a smell. It slows the suite, is non-deterministic (3 s might not be enough on slow CI), and blocks the thread.

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with back-off up to a bounded timeout
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
    {
        await Task.Delay(100, cts.Token);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures; 3 s of waste on every run (Camara et al. correlate Sleepy Test with flakiness).

---

### 🔴 HIGH — 2. Conditional Test Logic

**Academic name:** Conditional Test Logic  
**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 93–109)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

The `if/else` means **one branch never executes**. If `GetByEmail` always returns a regular `User`, the `PremiumUser` path is dead code that gives false coverage.

**Fix — write two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "Vip", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Hidden testing gap — one type path is never verified.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — 3. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Academic name:** Unknown Test / Empty Test  
**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // No assertions — passes even if Insert silently fails
}
```

The name suggests "runs without errors", but if `Insert` silently swallows failures or inserts nothing, this test still passes.

**Fix — assert the outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100% pass rate with 0% behavioral verification.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — 4. Assertion Roulette

**Academic name:** Assertion Roulette  
**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38), `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` (lines 51–53), `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` (lines 76–77), `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` (line 132)

Example from `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);          // line 35 — no message
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);  // line 36 — no message
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email); // line 37 — no message
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);       // line 38 — no message
```

Four assertions, zero explanatory messages. When one fails in CI, the stack trace gives only a line number — you must open the file to understand.

**Fix — add assertion messages (at least for the 3+ assertion methods):**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip through the database");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip through the database");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slow failure diagnosis in CI — developers waste time mapping line numbers to meaning.

---

## Smells NOT Found (Clean Patterns ✅)

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| **Mystery Guest** | ✅ Clean — `SqliteConnection("Data Source=:memory:")` is an in-memory database, not an external resource. Correctly hermetic for integration tests. |
| **Eager Test** | ✅ Clean — each test calls a focused workflow (insert→get, insert→update→get, insert→delete→get). Multiple calls are the natural arrange-act-assert for integration tests. |
| **Sensitive Equality** | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()`-based comparisons found. |
| **Exception Handling** | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` blocks in any test. |
| **Magic Number Test** | ✅ Clean — `3` in `ListUsers` is self-documenting (3 items inserted → count = 3); `10` and `100` follow the same pattern. |
| **General Fixture** | ✅ Clean — `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test method. |
| **Ignored/Disabled Test** | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` annotations. |
| **Duplicate Assert** | ✅ Clean — no repeated identical assertions. |
| **Lazy Test** | ✅ Clean — each test targets a distinct scenario. |
| **Redundant Print** | ✅ Clean — no `Console.WriteLine` artifacts. |
| **Redundant Assertion** | ✅ Clean — no tautological `Assert.IsTrue(true)`. |
| **Default Test** | ✅ Clean — class is properly named. |
| **Constructor Initialization** | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]`, not a constructor. |
| **Resource Optimism** | ✅ Clean — in-memory DB requires no external resources. |
| **Empty Test** | ✅ Clean — all tests have executable code. |

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|--------|
| **1** | Assertion-Free Test (`BulkInsert`) | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` | Eliminates false-pass risk | ~2 min |
| **2** | Conditional Test Logic (`GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`) | Split into two tests | Eliminates hidden dead branch | ~10 min |
| **3** | Sleepy Test (`NotifyOnInsert`) | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop | Eliminates flakiness + saves 3 s/run | ~10 min |
| **4** | Assertion Roulette (4 tests) | Add message parameters to assertions | Faster failure diagnosis | ~5 min |

**Bottom line:** The suite's architecture is solid — hermetic in-memory DB, proper setup/teardown, focused test methods. The three high-severity smells are localized and quick to fix. Address priorities 1–3 and your suite goes from "arguing about" to "confidently citing" in under 30 minutes.
